WEBVTT - Surveyor 3: The Little Explorer That Made History (Master Documentary Subtitles)

1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Imagine a world where the sky is always black,
where footprints can last for millions of years...

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:13.200
...and where every rock has a story to tell.
This is the Moon.

3
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:22.136
Before humans explored its surface, NASA sent a series of robotic explorers
to discover what was waiting there.

4
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.800
On April 20, 1967, NASA launched a three-legged robotic scout named Surveyor 3.

5
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:39.500
Unlike modern rovers with wheels, Surveyor was designed to land softly
and remain where it touched down.

6
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:48.048
It carried sensitive cameras and mechanical tools to answer one crucial question:
Could the lunar surface support the weight of an Apollo landing craft?

7
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.800
Thousands of scientists and engineers watched with held breath
as telemetry streamed into Mission Control.

8
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:07.168
Millions of miles away, the robotic explorer faced a terrifying challenge:
descending through the lunar vacuum and surviving touchdown.

9
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:23.500
On April 20, 1967, Surveyor 3 approached the Moon at supersonic speed.

10
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:31.200
Solid retro-rockets fired to slow its fall, but radar glitches
prevented the vernier engines from shutting down at the expected moment.

11
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:40.568
The spacecraft bounced once, twice, and finally settled safely
into the gentle slope of a lunar crater.

12
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:54.200
Perhaps Surveyor's most crucial experiment was its surface electrical sampler:
a motorized scissor pantograph with an articulated scoop.

13
00:01:54.600 --> 00:02:02.500
Controlled remotely by engineers on Earth, it extended out onto the regolith,
digging four separate trenches up to seven inches deep...

14
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:10.712
...proving that lunar soil could bear the weight of human astronauts.

15
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:29.800
Over the next two weeks, Surveyor 3 beamed back 6,315 high-resolution
television pictures of Oceanus Procellarum.

16
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:39.500
Its rotating beryllium mirror scanned 360 degrees across the crater floor and walls,
documenting rocks, secondary craters, and soil textures.

17
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:48.808
It even captured a historic view of a solar eclipse from the lunar surface,
as Earth passed directly in front of the blinding sun.

18
00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:03.200
On May 4, 1967, the sun set over Surveyor Crater.
In the bitter cold of the lunar night, temperatures plummeted to minus 250 degrees Fahrenheit.

19
00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:12.500
The solar panel lost its light, and Surveyor's battery drained to zero.
Its final telemetry signal faded into static.

20
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:21.604
Alone in the freezing vacuum, the silent machine began a long wait.

21
00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:34.000
Thirty-one months later, in November 1969, Apollo 12 reached the Moon.
Commander Pete Conrad and lunar module pilot Alan Bean performed the first pinpoint human landing in history.

22
00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Bringing the lunar module Intrepid down on the crater rim, Conrad looked out the window and cheered:
"Houston, we're on the ground, right on target!"

23
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:54.952
Just six hundred feet away, resting in the shadows,
was their target: Surveyor 3.

24
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:09.500
On November 19, Conrad and Bean walked down into the crater.
For the first time in human history, humans visited a machine previously sent to another world.

25
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:18.000
The astronauts photographed the lander, examined its dust-covered structure...

26
00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:26.032
...and carefully cut away its television camera, soil scoop,
and polished aluminum tubing to bring back to Earth.

27
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:45.500
Back on Earth, scientists studied the returned components
to understand how long-term exposure to the harsh space environment affects human technology.

28
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:57.872
Surveyor 3 was more than just a robotic scout.
It was the little explorer that proved humans could touch another world,
paving the way for the greatest journeys in human history.
